HARC: A Theoretical Framework for Human–AI Cooperative Sequential Diagnosis under Trajectory-Level Chance Constraints

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 


Abstract

This dissertation develops HARC (Human–AI Rapid Cooperative Diagnosis), a theoretical framework for sequential fault diagnosis in safety-critical engineered systems in which an artificial agent cooperates with a human decision-maker under a trajectory-level chance constraint. The problem is cast as a mixed-initiative chance-constrained partially observable Markov decision process (CC-POMDP) with three structural features that distinguish it from existing formulations: (i) the human is modeled as a categorical stochastic policy \pi_H(a^H \mid z, a^R) = \text{Cat}(a^H; \boldsymbol{\mu}(z, a^R)) with Dirichlet belief over \boldsymbol{\mu}, conditioned on observable context rather than hidden state; (ii) human actions execute after a deterministic FIFO delay, so the executed action, not the newly issued one, enters the transition kernel; and (iii) the safety constraint is enforced by a projected adaptive threshold that guarantees the budget invariant almost surely on every trajectory, not merely in long-run average.

The central theoretical results are four. First, a fixed-budget soundness theorem establishes that any policy selecting actions from \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}^{\text{fix}}(b_t) = \{a : P(F \mid b_t, a) \le \Delta_t\} with \sum_t \Delta_t \le \varepsilon satisfies P_\pi(\bigcup_{t=0}^{T} F_t \mid b_0) \le \varepsilon by the union bound. Second, a projected adaptive soundness theorem extends this to belief-dependent thresholds \Delta'(b_t) = \min\{\tilde{\Delta}(b_t), (\varepsilon - \text{spent}_t)/(T-t+1)\}, proving that the projection enforces the budget invariant at every step and that adaptive redistribution never breaches the global budget. Third, a simulation lemma for perturbed POMDPs yields the robust bound P_{\mathcal{M}^*}(\bigcup_t F_t) \le \varepsilon + T\eta, stating all guarantees are model-relative and giving the model-adequacy gate a quantitative role. Fourth, a mapping of the diagnostic loop onto active sequential hypothesis testing yields the efficiency bound \mathbb{E}[T_{\text{resolution}}] \le \log(1/\varepsilon)/I^* + O(\log M) + \mathbb{E}[T_{\text{human-delay}}], where I^* is the minimum Chernoff information over hypothesis pairs.

Three failure events are modeled as distinct and non-interchangeable: wrong-commit, test-induced, and delay-induced. A pre-posterior feasible set replaces per-belief admissibility, restoring non-negativity of the expected value of sample information and eliminating the artifact of negative EVSI. Common-cause failure between the two verification channels is parameterized by a measurable factor \beta \in (0,1) rather than asserted by design, with the bound P(\text{both err}) \le (1-\beta)^2 p_e^2 + \beta p_e. The model-adequacy gate is presented honestly as a heuristic with empirically tuned thresholds, not a formal hypothesis test, because the posterior predictive p-value concentrates around 1/2 under a composite null rather than being uniform.

The framework is theoretical: no experiments, no physical tests, no code training. All results are theorems, propositions, and proofs. A Monte Carlo validation protocol with synthetic humans is specified to test the soundness theorems and the robust bound in the absence of physical deployment.

Keywords: human-in-the-loop CC-POMDP, mixed-initiative Bayes-adaptive POMDP, sequential diagnosis, chance-constrained planning, value of information, active sequential hypothesis testing, Chernoff information, model adequacy, common-cause failure.

