Chapter 0: Introduction

0.1 The Problem

Consider an engineer responsible for a safety-critical subsystem — a spacecraft power bus, a nuclear reactor coolant loop, an autonomous vehicle perception stack — who must diagnose a fault under partial observability, a hard deadline, and a risk of making the fault worse. The engineer does not observe the true fault. Each test consumes time and resources, and some tests can themselves induce failure. A wrong conclusion acted upon may be catastrophic. A correct conclusion reached too late may be worthless. The engineer may be aided by an artificial agent that proposes hypotheses, selects tests, and recommends actions, but the human retains authority, and the human's latency, error, and compliance behavior are part of the system dynamics.

This is the setting of HARC. The problem is formally a sequential decision problem under partial observability with a trajectory-level safety constraint and a human decision-maker whose policy is unknown, stochastic, and estimated online. It is not a single-agent problem. The human is a decision-theoretic agent whose actions affect the joint trajectory, and any framework that treats the human as a passive confirmer will produce unsound safety claims.

0.2 Why Existing Frameworks Are Insufficient

The relevant prior art divides into five strands, none of which addresses the joint problem.

Model-based diagnosis — the DX tradition initiated by de Kleer and Williams (1987) and extended to reactive systems by Williams and Nayak (1996) — provides the logical and probabilistic machinery for generating and eliminating candidate diagnoses from observations. It does not address test selection under a chance constraint, nor does it model a human decision-maker.

Test sequencing — the AO* framework of Pattipati and Alexandridis (1990) — provides optimal sequential test selection under a cost criterion. It is single-agent, assumes perfect tests, and does not enforce trajectory-level safety constraints.

Chance-constrained POMDP planning — RAO* (Santana, Thiébaux, and Williams, 2016), CC-POMCP (Lee et al., 2018), and ConstrainedZero (Moss et al., 2024) — provides the algorithmic foundation for planning under a trajectory-level chance constraint. CC-POMCP bounds expected cost, not chance; RAO* and ConstrainedZero bound chance but assume a single decision-maker. None models a human-in-the-loop with latency, error, and compliance dynamics.

Learning-to-defer — Madras et al. (2018), Mozannar and Sontag (2020) — provides the statistical machinery for routing decisions between an AI and a human expert. It is a single-step classification framework, not a sequential decision problem under partial observability, and it does not model the human's internal state or the delay between deferral and response.

Human–AI teaming — the mixed-initiative Bayes-adaptive POMDP of Natarajan et al. (2024) — provides the correct object for maintaining belief over an uncertain human policy: the augmented state (s, z, \mu) with online Bayesian updates. It does not enforce trajectory-level chance constraints and does not address the diagnostic setting with distinct failure modes.

HARC's contribution is the joint treatment of: the human as a categorical exogenous policy with FIFO delay and Dirichlet belief; the three distinct failure events with a trajectory-level chance constraint; the projected adaptive threshold that preserves soundness under adaptivity; the pre-posterior EVSI that restores non-negativity; and the mapping to active sequential hypothesis testing that yields a resolution-time bound in terms of Chernoff information. The novelty is the coupling, not any single component, and it is stated as a hypothesis to be examined rather than a conclusion.

0.3 Contributions

This dissertation makes six contributions.

Contribution 1 (Categorical human model with observable-context Dirichlet belief). The human policy is modeled as

\pi_H(a^H \mid z, a^R) = \text{Cat}(a^H; \boldsymbol{\mu}(z, a^R)), \qquad \boldsymbol{\mu}(z, a^R) \sim \text{Dirichlet}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}(z, a^R)),

with the human action space A^H = \{\text{accept}, \text{reject}, \text{propose-alt}, \text{request-info}, \text{defer}, \text{no-op}\}. The Dirichlet is maintained per observable context, not per hidden (s, z), because the AI never observes s and cannot accumulate counts per hidden cell. The AI's proposal a^R is a covariate of the human policy, not a hidden state.

Contribution 2 (FIFO-delay dynamics with executed action). The augmented state includes a queue q_t \in (A^H)^{d-1}, and the executed action at step t is a^{\text{exec}}_t = \text{pop}(q_t). The joint transition feeds the executed action into p(s' \mid s, a^R, a^{\text{exec}}), not the newly issued action. The queue capacity d is a fixed design parameter, not a per-step random variable.

Contribution 3 (Two-level risk constraint with projected adaptive threshold). Fixed-budget soundness (Theorem 4.1) establishes P_\pi(\bigcup_t F_t \mid b_0) \le \varepsilon via the union bound. Projected adaptive soundness (Theorem 4.2) extends this to belief-dependent thresholds \Delta'(b_t) = \min\{\tilde{\Delta}(b_t), (\varepsilon - \text{spent}_t)/(T-t+1)\}, proving the budget invariant holds at every step. Adaptive conformal inference is a diagnostic for choosing \tilde{\Delta}, not a soundness mechanism.

Contribution 4 (Robust soundness via simulation lemma). A simulation lemma for perturbed POMDPs yields \|\mathbb{P}_\mathcal{M}^\pi - \mathbb{P}_{\hat{\mathcal{M}}}^\pi\|_{\text{TV}} \le T\eta, from which the robust bound P_{\mathcal{M}^*}(\bigcup_t F_t) \le \varepsilon + T\eta follows. This gives the model-adequacy gate a quantitative role: it estimates \eta, and the guarantee is stated as model-relative.

Contribution 5 (Pre-posterior EVSI and the elimination of negative EVSI). The pre-posterior feasible set

\mathcal{D}_{\text{pre}}(b) = \{d \in \mathcal{D} : \mathbb{E}_{o \mid b, a}[P(F \mid b', d)] \le \Delta'(b)\}

replaces per-belief admissibility. Under the pre-posterior constraint, EVSI is non-negative by the tower property, and the artifact of negative EVSI from per-belief admissibility is eliminated. A safe default decision is always feasible, so \max over the empty set does not occur.

Contribution 6 (Active sequential hypothesis testing and the Chernoff bound). The diagnostic loop is mapped onto active sequential hypothesis testing. The accept predicate is proved to be a threshold rule, and the expected resolution time satisfies

\mathbb{E}[T_{\text{resolution}}] \le \frac{\log(1/\varepsilon)}{I^*} + O(\log M) + \mathbb{E}[T_{\text{human-delay}}],

where I^* is the minimum Chernoff information over hypothesis pairs. This gives "faster at equal safety" a theoretical footing and fills a prior-art gap.

0.4 Two Structural Corrections

Two corrections distinguish this formulation from earlier versions of the framework and are load-bearing for the soundness results.

Correction A (Adaptive soundness requires projection, not conformal inference). The earlier use of adaptive conformal inference as the soundness mechanism was incorrect. Adaptive conformal inference guarantees long-run marginal coverage, not trajectory-wise coverage. The two guarantees are distinct: the former is a statement about the empirical frequency of failures over many episodes, the latter about the probability of at least one failure in a single episode. The soundness mechanism must be a projection that enforces the budget invariant at every step, and adaptive conformal inference is relegated to a diagnostic for choosing the unconstrained threshold.

Correction B (Common-cause failure is parametric, not axiomatic). The earlier assertion that the two verification channels are independent "by design" was unfalsifiable and contradicted by the observation that the hypothesis-generation channel is LLM-driven. The correct formulation is parametric: a common-cause factor \beta \in (0,1) captures shared causes such as shared sensors, shared FMEA, and shared training data, and the theorems are stated parametrically in \beta. Typical conservative values are \beta \in [0.05, 0.10].

0.5 Method and Scope

The dissertation is theoretical. No experiments are performed, no physical tests are conducted, and no code is trained. All results are theorems, propositions, and proofs. The architecture is a design specification. A Monte Carlo validation protocol with synthetic humans is specified to test the soundness theorems and the robust bound in the absence of physical deployment.

The scope is deliberately narrowed. The full HARC architecture comprises seven modules (M0–M6). The theoretical development covers all seven at the specification level, but the depth of formal treatment is concentrated on the decision-theoretic core: the human model, the risk projection, the pre-posterior EVSI, the common-cause parameterization, the model-adequacy gate, and the Chernoff bound. Module-level implementation details — the LLM calibration procedure, the digital-twin interface, the evidence-memory schema — are specified but not proved.

0.6 Organization

Chapter 2 establishes the mathematical background: POMDPs, chance-constrained POMDPs, the simulation lemma, and the scope and limits of adaptive conformal inference. Chapter 3 defines the HARC model: the augmented state, the categorical human model, the FIFO queue, the failure events, and the parametric common-cause factor. Chapter 4 develops the two-level risk constraint: fixed-budget soundness and projected adaptive soundness with the budget invariant. Chapter 5 develops the decision-theoretic layer: the pre-posterior feasible set, EVSI, and lookahead-2 EVSI. Chapter 6 treats common-cause failure parametrically. Chapter 7 analyzes the model-adequacy gate. Chapter 8 specifies the architecture. Chapter 9 develops the HARC policy, with totality, progress, and soundness. Chapter 10 analyzes complexity. Chapter 11 develops the active sequential hypothesis testing theorem. Chapter 12 specifies the Monte Carlo validation protocol. Chapter 13 lists open problems. Chapter 14 concludes. Appendices contain full proofs, derivations, and specifications.

0.7 The Founding Principle

The framework is motivated by a single principle, which the remainder of the dissertation formalizes:

AI should accelerate the process that distinguishes a promising solution from a genuinely verified one — and it must know when it cannot.

The first clause motivates the test-selection layer and the Chernoff bound. The second clause motivates the model-adequacy gate, the robust soundness bound, and the three-valued output. The two together motivate the projected adaptive threshold: the system should use its budget aggressively when evidence supports it, and it should stop when the budget is exhausted, the model is inadequate, or no safe action remains.



Chapter 1: Problem Formulation and Mathematical Background

1.1 The Diagnostic Decision Problem

We consider an AI agent cooperating with a human decision-maker to diagnose and resolve faults in a safety-critical engineered system. The system evolves under partial observability, the diagnosis is not directly observable, and the resolution process is subject to a hard deadline. Three classes of failure may occur: acting on a wrong diagnosis, damaging the system through the act of testing, and exceeding the deadline while a decision is required. The human is a decision-theoretic agent whose policy is unknown, stochastic, and estimated online.

1.2 Preliminaries: POMDPs, Chance Constraints, and the Simulation Lemma

Definition 1.1 (POMDP). A partially observable Markov decision process is a tuple \mathcal{P} = \langle S, A, O, T, Z, R, \gamma, b_0 \rangle.

Definition 1.2 (Chance-Constrained POMDP). A CC-POMDP augments a POMDP with a failure event F and budget \varepsilon \in (0,1):

\min_{\pi} \; \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T} \gamma^t R(s_t, a_t)\right] \quad \text{s.t.} \quad P_\pi\left(\bigcup_{t=0}^{T} F_t \mid b_0\right) \le \varepsilon

Definition 1.3 (Simulation Lemma for POMDPs). Let \mathcal{M} and \hat{\mathcal{M}} be two POMDPs with the same state, action, and observation spaces, but different transition and observation kernels. Let \pi be a fixed policy. Let \mathbb{P}_\mathcal{M}^\pi and \mathbb{P}_{\hat{\mathcal{M}}}^\pi be the induced path measures over horizon T. Then

\| \mathbb{P}_\mathcal{M}^\pi - \mathbb{P}_{\hat{\mathcal{M}}}^\pi \|_{\text{TV}} \le T \eta

where \eta = \sup_{s,a} \| T(\cdot \mid s, a) - \hat{T}(\cdot \mid s, a) \|_{\text{TV}} + \sup_{s',a} \| Z(\cdot \mid s', a) - \hat{Z}(\cdot \mid s', a) \|_{\text{TV}}.

Recent work on POMDP robustness provides explicit bounds on value differences under simultaneous transition and observation kernel perturbations, in terms of Wasserstein and total variation distances. The simulation lemma turns per-step model error into a value-error bound that scales with the horizon.

Corollary 1.1 (Robust Soundness). If the true model differs from the nominal model by total variation \eta per step, then any guarantee established under the nominal model transfers to the true model with an additive slack of T \eta:

P_{\mathcal{M}^*}\left(\bigcup_{t=0}^{T} F_t\right) \le \varepsilon + T \eta

This is the formal basis for all model-relative guarantees in HARC.

1.3 Adaptive Conformal Inference: Scope and Limits

Definition 1.4 (Adaptive Conformal Inference). Under distribution shift, Gibbs & Candès propose the update \alpha_{t+1} = \alpha_t + \gamma_t (\alpha - \mathbf{1}\{Y_t \notin \mathcal{C}_t(X_t)\}).

Theorem 1.1 (Long-run coverage; Gibbs & Candès, 2021). Under bounded scores and suitable \gamma_t, the adaptive conformal predictor satisfies

\left| \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathbf{1}\{Y_t \notin \mathcal{C}_t(X_t)\} - \alpha \right| \to 0 \quad \text{a.s.}

Corollary 1.2 (Non-Transferability). The long-run average guarantee of Theorem 1.1 does not imply trajectory-wise coverage P(\bigcup_{t=0}^{T} F_t) \le \varepsilon for any finite T. The two guarantees are distinct. ACI is a calibration diagnostic, not a soundness mechanism.

