Chapter 2: The HARC Formal Model

2.1 The Mixed-Initiative Tuple

Definition 2.1 (HARC Model).

\mathcal{M} = \langle S, A, O, T, Z, R, C, \varepsilon, b_0, H, \beta \rangle

· S = S_{\text{diag}} \times S_{\text{sys}} \times S_{\text{human}} \times S_{\text{time}} \times S_{\text{risk}} \times Q: augmented state;
· A = A_{\text{AI}} \cup A_{\text{joint}}: AI action space;
· T: transition kernel on the augmented state;
· Z: observation likelihood, anchored on FMEA, fault trees, and digital twins;
· R: S \times A \to \mathbb{R}: reward;
· C: S \times A \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}: cost;
· \varepsilon \in (0,1): trajectory-level chance budget;
· H = \langle S_h, O_h, T_h, \pi_H \rangle: human model;
· \beta \in (0,1): common-cause failure factor (Section 2.7).

2.2 Augmented State Space

S = S_{\text{diag}} \times S_{\text{sys}} \times S_{\text{human}} \times S_{\text{time}} \times S_{\text{risk}} \times Q

· S_{\text{diag}}: true diagnosis;
· S_{\text{sys}}: physical system state;
· S_{\text{human}}: human latent state z = (\text{workload}, \text{attention}, \text{fatigue}, \text{expertise}, \text{trust});
· S_{\text{time}}: time-to-deadline T_{\text{remaining}} \in [0, T_{\max}];
· S_{\text{risk}}: cumulative incurred risk R_{\text{cum}} \in [0, \varepsilon];
· Q: FIFO action queue, q_t \in (A^H)^{d-1}.

Remark 2.1. The queue Q is essential. Without it, the belief cannot encode imminent human actions.

2.3 AI Action Space

A_{\text{AI}} = \{\text{propose}, \text{test}, \text{verify}, \text{hold}, \text{accept}, \text{abort}, \text{escalate}\}

A_{\text{joint}} = \{\text{collaborative-test}, \text{joint-review}\}

2.4 Human Model: Categorical Policy with Dirichlet Belief

Definition 2.2 (Human action space).

A^H = \{\text{accept}, \text{reject}, \text{propose-alt}, \text{request-info}, \text{defer}, \text{no-op}\}

with |A^H| = K = 6. The no-op action covers cases where the human does not respond.

Definition 2.3 (Human policy). The human policy conditions on the AI's proposal a^R:

\pi_H(a^H \mid s, z, a^R) = \text{Cat}(a^H; \boldsymbol{\mu}(s, z, a^R))

Remark 2.2. The AI's proposal is a covariate, not a hidden state. Conditioning on a^R is required for the human to respond to what the AI proposes.

Definition 2.4 (Dirichlet belief). The AI maintains a Dirichlet prior over \boldsymbol{\mu} for each observable context (z, a^R), not for each hidden (s, z):

\boldsymbol{\mu}(z, a^R) \sim \text{Dirichlet}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}(z, a^R))

Remark 2.3. A Dirichlet per hidden (s, z) cell is not learnable: the AI never observes s, so it cannot accumulate counts per hidden state. The correct object is a Dirichlet per observable context. The concentration parameters \boldsymbol{\alpha} are the sufficient statistics, and the counts are observed, so \boldsymbol{\alpha} in the belief is redundant with the history.

Proposition 2.1 (Conjugacy). The Dirichlet prior is conjugate to the Categorical likelihood. Upon observing human action a^H = k in context (z, a^R), the posterior is \boldsymbol{\alpha}(z, a^R) \leftarrow \boldsymbol{\alpha}(z, a^R) + \mathbf{e}_k.

Proof. Standard Dirichlet-Categorical conjugacy. The posterior predictive is P(a^H = k \mid z, a^R, \boldsymbol{\alpha}) = \alpha_k / \sum_j \alpha_j. \square

2.5 Delay Dynamics: Executed Action, Not Issued Action

Definition 2.5 (Queue dynamics). The queue evolves as:

q_{t+1} = \text{shift}(q_t, a^H_t)

where \text{shift} appends a^H_t to the queue and pops the oldest element if the queue exceeds capacity d-1.

Definition 2.6 (Executed action). The executed human action at step t is the element popped from the queue:

a^{\text{exec}}_t = \text{pop}(q_t)

where \text{pop} returns the oldest element of the queue.

Remark 2.4. The joint transition must feed the executed action into p(s' \mid \cdot), not the newly issued action:

T(s', z', q' \mid s, z, q, a^R) = \sum_{a^H} p(s' \mid s, a^R, a^{\text{exec}}) \, p(z' \mid z, a^R, a^{\text{exec}}) \, \pi_H(a^H \mid z, a^R) \, \mathbf{1}\{q' = \text{shift}(q, a^H)\}

Remark 2.5. The delay d is a fixed queue capacity, not a random variable per step. A random d that changes per step would break FIFO. The queue length is a design parameter of the human model.

2.6 Failure Events

Definition 2.7 (Failure events).

F_t = F_t^{\text{wrong-commit}} \cup F_t^{\text{test-induced}} \cup F_t^{\text{delay-induced}}

· F_t^{\text{wrong-commit}}: acting on d \ne d^* with U(d^*, s) - U(d, s) > \theta_{\text{crit}};
· F_t^{\text{test-induced}}: test a causes s' \in S_{\text{crit}};
· F_t^{\text{delay-induced}}: T_{\text{remaining}} < 0 while a decision is required.

Definition 2.8 (Decision required). A decision is required at state s if inaction leads to s' \in S_{\text{crit}} within T_{\text{remaining}}.

2.7 Common-Cause Failure: Parametric β Model

Definition 2.9 (Two verification channels). Channel 1 (M2, Hypothesis Explorer) uses observation model Z_1. Channel 2 (M3, Adversarial Examiner) uses observation model Z_2.

Definition 2.10 (Common-cause factor). The common-cause factor \beta \in (0,1) is the fraction of total failure probability attributable to shared causes:

P(\text{both channels err} \mid s) \le (1 - \beta)^2 p_e^2 + \beta p_e

where p_e is the per-channel error probability.

Proposition 2.2 (Parametric bound). Under the \beta-factor model, the joint error probability is bounded by (1-\beta)^2 p_e^2 + \beta p_e.

Proof. By the law of total probability, decompose the event "both err" into the disjoint cases "shared cause occurs" (probability \beta) and "shared cause does not occur" (probability 1-\beta). In the shared-cause case, both channels err with probability at most p_e (the common cause drives the failure). In the independent case, conditional independence gives (1-\beta)^2 p_e^2. Summing yields the bound. \square

Remark 2.6. The \beta-factor model is standard in common-cause failure analysis. Typical conservative values are \beta \in [0.05, 0.10]. The parameter \beta is measurable in principle from failure data, or bounded conservatively by design. This is preferable to the untestable "by design" axiom: it yields parametric theorems and makes the shared-cause assumption explicit.

Remark 2.7. Z_1 is LLM-generated and Z_2 is digital-twin-derived. Shared sensors and FMEA are common causes. The \beta parameter captures them.

