Chapter 3: The Belief State and its Dynamics

3.1 Belief Representation

Definition 3.1 (Belief).

b_t(s, z, \boldsymbol{\alpha}, q) = P(s_t = s, z_t = z, \boldsymbol{\alpha}_t = \boldsymbol{\alpha}, q_t = q \mid h_t)

Remark 3.1. The evidence memory is not in the hidden state. It is the history h_t.

3.2 Belief Update

Definition 3.2 (Belief update).

b_{t+1}(\tilde{s}') \propto Z(o_{t+1} \mid \tilde{s}', a^R_t) \sum_{\tilde{s}} T(\tilde{s}' \mid \tilde{s}, a^R_t) \, b_t(\tilde{s})

where \tilde{s} = (s, z, \boldsymbol{\alpha}, q).

Proposition 3.1 (Sufficiency). The belief b_t is a sufficient statistic for h_t.

Proof. The belief is a function of h_t and encodes all information relevant to future transitions and observations. By the Markov property of the augmented state, b_t satisfies the Bellman optimality principle. \square

