Chapter 4: Two-Level Risk Constraint with Hard Soundness

This chapter resolves the central formal issue: the conflation of marginal long-run coverage with trajectory-wise soundness. We separate the two guarantees and prove soundness via projection.

4.1 Fixed-Budget Soundness

Definition 4.1 (Fixed risk allocation). Let \{\Delta_t\}_{t=0}^{T} be a fixed allocation with \sum_{t=0}^{T} \Delta_t \le \varepsilon, \Delta_t > 0.

Definition 4.2 (Safe action set, fixed).

\mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}^{\text{fix}}(b_t) = \{a \in A : P(F \mid b_t, a) \le \Delta_t\}

Theorem 4.1 (Fixed-budget soundness). If the policy selects a_t \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}^{\text{fix}}(b_t) for all t, then

P_\pi\left(\bigcup_{t=0}^{T} F_t \mid b_0\right) \le \varepsilon

Proof. By the union bound,

P\left(\bigcup_{t=0}^{T} F_t \mid b_0\right) \le \sum_{t=0}^{T} P(F_t \mid b_t, a_t) \le \sum_{t=0}^{T} \Delta_t \le \varepsilon

The second inequality uses the safe-action constraint. \square

4.2 Projected Adaptive Threshold

The fixed allocation is sound but potentially suboptimal. We introduce an adaptive threshold that redistributes unused budget, with a projection that enforces the budget invariant.

Definition 4.3 (Spent risk). The spent risk at step t is

\text{spent}_t = \sum_{s=0}^{t-1} \hat{P}(F_s \mid b_s, a_s)

where \hat{P}(F_s \mid b_s, a_s) is the estimated failure probability at step s.

Definition 4.4 (Projected adaptive threshold). The adaptive threshold is

\Delta'(b_t) = \min\left\{ \tilde{\Delta}(b_t), \; \frac{\varepsilon - \text{spent}_t}{T - t + 1} \right\}

where \tilde{\Delta}(b_t) is an unconstrained adaptation (e.g., ACI-style update).

Remark 4.1. The projection enforces \Delta'(b_t) \le (\varepsilon - \text{spent}_t)/(T - t + 1). This prevents the adaptive rule from driving \Delta' negative or past the remaining budget.

Definition 4.5 (Budget invariant). The budget invariant holds at step t if

\text{spent}_t + (T - t + 1) \cdot \Delta'(b_t) \le \varepsilon

Lemma 4.1 (Projection enforces invariant). Under Definition 4.4, the budget invariant holds at every step t.

Proof. By construction,

\Delta'(b_t) \le \frac{\varepsilon - \text{spent}_t}{T - t + 1}

Multiplying both sides by T - t + 1 and adding \text{spent}_t gives the invariant. \square

Definition 4.6 (Safe action set, adaptive).

\mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}(b_t) = \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}^{\text{fix}}(b_t) \cap \{a : P(F \mid b_t, a) \le \Delta'(b_t)\}

Theorem 4.2 (Adaptive soundness with projection). If the policy selects a_t \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}(b_t) for all t, then

P_\pi\left(\bigcup_{t=0}^{T} F_t \mid b_0\right) \le \varepsilon

Proof. By Lemma 4.1, the budget invariant holds at every step. At step t, the safe-action constraint gives P(F_t \mid b_t, a_t) \le \Delta'(b_t). Summing over t:

\sum_{t=0}^{T} P(F_t \mid b_t, a_t) \le \sum_{t=0}^{T} \Delta'(b_t)

By the invariant at t=0, \text{spent}_0 = 0 and (T+1)\Delta'(b_0) \le \varepsilon, so \sum_{t=0}^{T} \Delta'(b_t) \le \varepsilon. The union bound gives the result. \square

Remark 4.2. ACI drops out of the soundness path. The projection is the soundness mechanism; ACI is only a heuristic for choosing \tilde{\Delta}.

Corollary 4.1 (Robust soundness). Under model mismatch \eta, the true-model guarantee is

P_{\mathcal{M}^*}\left(\bigcup_{t=0}^{T} F_t\right) \le \varepsilon + T \eta

Proof. By the simulation lemma (Definition 1.3) and Corollary 1.1. \square

Remark 4.3. This gives M0 a quantitative role: M0 estimates \eta, and the bound states that all guarantees are model-relative.

4.3 ACI as Diagnostic Only

Theorem 4.3 (Marginal coverage; Gibbs & Candès, 2021). Under suitable \gamma_t, \left| \frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \mathbf{1}\{F_t\} - \alpha \right| \to 0 a.s.

Corollary 4.2 (Non-transferability). Theorem 4.3 does not imply the trajectory-wise bound of Theorem 4.2. The two guarantees are distinct.

Remark 4.4. Failures are too rare to serve as useful diagnostics for ACI in the safety-critical regime. ACI is reported as a calibration diagnostic, not a soundness guarantee.

