Chapter 5: Decision-Theoretic Layer — Pre-Posterior Constraints and EVSI

5.1 Pre-Posterior Constraint

Definition 5.1 (Pre-posterior feasible set). The pre-posterior feasible set at belief b is

\mathcal{D}_{\text{pre}}(b) = \left\{ d \in \mathcal{D} : \mathbb{E}_{o \mid b, a}\left[ P(F \mid b', d) \right] \le \Delta'(b) \right\}

where b' = \tau(b, a, o) is the posterior.

Remark 5.1. The pre-posterior constraint ensures that the expected failure probability under the posterior is bounded. This avoids the artifact of per-belief admissibility, which is stricter than the average (by the tower property) and can artificially create negative EVSI.

5.2 EVSI under Pre-Posterior Constraint

Definition 5.2 (Pre-posterior EVSI).

\boxed{
\operatorname{EVSI}(a) = \mathbb{E}_{o \mid b, a}\left[ \max_{d \in \mathcal{D}_{\text{pre}}(b')} \mathbb{E}_{s \mid b'}[U(d, s)] \right] - \max_{d \in \mathcal{D}_{\text{pre}}(b)} \mathbb{E}_{s \mid b}[U(d, s)]
}

Remark 5.2. EVSI is non-negative under the pre-posterior constraint, by the tower property and Jensen's inequality. The artifact of negative EVSI from per-belief admissibility is eliminated.

Proposition 5.1 (Non-negativity). Under the pre-posterior constraint, \operatorname{EVSI}(a) \ge 0.

Proof. Let V(b') = \max_{d \in \mathcal{D}_{\text{pre}}(b')} \mathbb{E}_{s \mid b'}[U(d, s)]. By the tower property,

\mathbb{E}_{o \mid b, a}[V(b')] \ge \max_{d \in \mathcal{D}_{\text{pre}}(b)} \mathbb{E}_{o \mid b, a}\left[ \mathbb{E}_{s \mid b'}[U(d, s)] \right] = \max_{d \in \mathcal{D}_{\text{pre}}(b)} \mathbb{E}_{s \mid b}[U(d, s)]

The inequality holds because the maximum of the expectation is at least the expectation of the maximum. \square

Remark 5.3. The safe default decision d_{\text{safe}} is always in \mathcal{D}_{\text{pre}}(b), so \max over \emptyset does not occur.

5.3 Lookahead-2 EVSI

Definition 5.3 (Lookahead-2 EVSI).

\operatorname{EVSI}_2(a_1) = \max_{a_2} \mathbb{E}_{o_1 \mid b, a_1}\left[ \operatorname{EVSI}(a_2 \mid b') \right]

Remark 5.4. The inner EVSI is computed at the posterior b', not at the prior. The definition is corrected from previous versions.

