Chapter 7: Model-Adequacy Gate (M0)

7.1 Posterior Predictive Checks

Definition 7.1 (Discrepancy statistic). T(s) = \sup_{x} |F_{\text{pred}}(x) - F_{\text{obs}}(x)|.

Definition 7.2 (Posterior predictive p-value).

p_{\text{PPP}} = P\left(T(s^{\text{rep}}) \ge T(s^{\text{obs}}) \mid \mathcal{M}, b\right)

7.2 Distribution of p_{\text{PPP}}

Proposition 7.1 (Concentration under composite null). Under a composite null hypothesis with estimated parameters, p_{\text{PPP}} concentrates around 1/2, with less mass in both tails than a uniform distribution. It is not uniformly distributed.

Proof. Robins, van der Vaart, and Ventura (2000) show that under a composite null with an unknown nuisance parameter, the asymptotic distribution of p_{\text{PPP}} is stochastically concentrated around 1/2. \square

Corollary 7.1 (False-positive rate). The false-positive rate is lower than nominal, but the power to detect misspecification is also reduced.

7.3 M0 as Heuristic Gate

Definition 7.3 (M0 gate).

\text{M0}(b) = \begin{cases} \text{inadequate} & \text{if } p_{\text{PPP}} < \gamma_{\text{low}} \text{ or } p_{\text{PPP}} > \gamma_{\text{high}} \\ \text{adequate} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

Remark 7.1. M0 is a heuristic with empirically tuned thresholds. It is not a formal hypothesis test with guaranteed size. The thresholds are design parameters.

Corollary 7.2 (M0 role in robust soundness). M0 estimates the model mismatch \eta. If M0 triggers, the system outputs undemonstrated and falls back to human-only procedure. The robust bound of Corollary 4.1 states that guarantees are model-relative: P(\bigcup F_t) \le \varepsilon + T \eta.

