Chapter 9: The HARC Policy — Totality, Progress, and Soundness

9.1 Lexicographic Guard Structure

Definition 9.1 (HARC policy).

\pi_{\text{HARC}}(b) = \begin{cases}
a_{\text{abort}} & \text{if } \text{spent} + \Delta'(b) > \varepsilon \\
a_{\text{accept}} & \text{if } \operatorname{Accept}(b, \mathcal{E}) = 1 \\
a_{\text{escalate}} & \text{if } \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}(b) = \emptyset \\
a_{\text{human}} & \text{if } \text{awaiting queued human action} \\
a_{\text{test}}^* & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

Remark 9.1. hold is removed as a standalone branch. It is used only when awaiting a queued human action. This eliminates the reachability bug: hold no longer pre-empts human and test every time.

9.2 Policy Totality

Theorem 9.1 (Totality). For every belief b, \pi_{\text{HARC}}(b) \in A.

Proof. The guards partition the belief space. The first three branches cover abort, accept, and escalate. If none fires, \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}(b) \neq \emptyset, and either the system is awaiting a human action (branch 4) or it selects a_{\text{test}}^* (branch 5). \square

Remark 9.2. Totality is trivial for an if/else chain. The nontrivial property is progress.

9.3 Progress

Definition 9.2 (Progress). The policy makes progress if, for every belief b reachable under \pi_{\text{HARC}}, either the episode terminates (accept, escalate, or abort) within a bounded number of steps, or the belief strictly improves in the sense that the entropy of b decreases by at least \delta_{\text{prog}} > 0.

Theorem 9.2 (Eventual termination). Under the assumption that safe tests have strictly positive expected information gain and that \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}(b) \neq \emptyset for all reachable b until termination, the policy terminates within T_{\max} steps.

Proof. Each test with positive expected information gain strictly decreases the expected entropy of the belief. The entropy is bounded below by 0. Therefore, after a finite number of tests, either the Accept predicate is satisfied or the safe action set becomes empty, triggering escalation or abort. The time-to-deadline constraint ensures termination within T_{\max}. \square

Remark 9.3. The safe-fallback assumption is explicit: when \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}(b) = \emptyset, the policy escalates. This is the strongest assumption in the paper, and it interacts with delay-induced failure.

9.4 Soundness

Theorem 9.3 (Soundness). Under the budget invariant (Lemma 4.1) and the safe-action constraint a_t \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}(b_t),

P_{\pi_{\text{HARC}}}\left(\bigcup_{t=0}^{T} F_t \mid b_0\right) \le \varepsilon

Proof. By Theorem 4.2. \square

Remark 9.4. The safe-fallback assumption (that abort or escalate has negligible risk) is explicit. It is a design assumption, not a theorem.

