Chapter 10: Complexity and Tractability

10.1 EVSI Complexity

Proposition 10.1 (Lookahead-1 EVSI). With full belief enumeration, O(|O| \cdot |\mathcal{D}|) per action.

Proposition 10.2 (Lookahead-2 EVSI). With full belief enumeration,

O(|A|^2 \cdot |O|^2 \cdot |\mathcal{D}| + |A| \cdot |O| \cdot C_{\text{update}})

where C_{\text{update}} is the belief update cost.

Proof. For each outer action a_1 and observation o_1, update the belief (cost C_{\text{update}}). For each inner action a_2 and observation o_2, update again and evaluate the maximum. The |A|^2 and |O|^2 factors are both present. \square

Remark 10.1. Previous versions missed the |A|^2 factor and the belief-update cost.

10.2 Particle Belief

Definition 10.1 (Particle belief). \hat{b}_t(\tilde{s}) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} w^{(i)} \delta_{\tilde{s}^{(i)}}(\tilde{s}).

Proposition 10.3 (Particle complexity). With N particles, lookahead-2 EVSI requires

O(N \cdot |A|^2 \cdot |O|)

operations.

Remark 10.2. For |\mathcal{S}| = 10^3, |A| = 10, |O| = 10^2, full enumeration is 10^7 per step. Flight processors handle 10^7 ops/s trivially. The previous claim that 10^7 is infeasible was wrong.

10.3 Rare-Event Resolution

Remark 10.3. N = 100 particles cannot resolve per-step risks near 10^{-4}, which requires \gtrsim 1/\Delta samples or importance splitting. For rare-event estimation, use sequential Monte Carlo with importance splitting or exponential tilting.

Remark 10.4. The 0.1–1 Hz claim is removed. The complexity is stated in operations; the frequency depends on hardware.

