Chapter 11: A Nontrivial Theorem: Diagnosis as Active Sequential Hypothesis Testing

11.1 Problem Mapping

Definition 11.1 (Active sequential hypothesis testing). Let \mathcal{H} = \{h_1, \ldots, h_M\} be a finite hypothesis set. At each step, the agent selects an action a \in A, observes o \sim Z(o \mid h^*, a), and updates the posterior. The objective is to minimize the expected number of samples subject to a correctness constraint.

This is the classical active sequential hypothesis testing problem of Wald and Chernoff. The HARC diagnostic loop is an instance: hypotheses are fault modes, actions are tests, observations are test outcomes.

11.2 Accept Rule as Threshold

Proposition 11.1 (Accept as threshold). Under the pre-posterior constraint, the Accept predicate is a threshold rule: there exists a threshold \theta_{\text{acc}} such that

\operatorname{Accept}(b) = 1 \iff \max_i P(h_i \mid b) \ge \theta_{\text{acc}}

Proof. The Accept predicate requires residual risk P(F \mid b) \le \Delta'(b). Under the mapping P(F \mid b) = 1 - \max_i P(h_i \mid b), the condition becomes \max_i P(h_i \mid b) \ge 1 - \Delta'(b). Set \theta_{\text{acc}} = 1 - \Delta'(b). \square

11.3 Expected Resolution Time

Definition 11.2 (Chernoff information). For two hypotheses h_i, h_j,

I_{ij} = \min_{a} \max_{\lambda \in [0,1]} \left[ -\log \sum_o Z(o \mid h_i, a)^\lambda Z(o \mid h_j, a)^{1-\lambda} \right]

Theorem 11.1 (Expected resolution time). Under the pre-posterior constraint and the threshold accept rule, the expected resolution time is bounded by

\mathbb{E}[T_{\text{resolution}}] \le \frac{\log(1/\varepsilon)}{I^*} + \mathbb{E}[T_{\text{human-delay}}]

where I^* = \min_{i \neq j} I_{ij} is the minimum Chernoff information and \mathbb{E}[T_{\text{human-delay}}] is the expected human latency overhead.

Proof sketch. By the Chernoff–Stein lemma, the number of samples required to distinguish h_i from h_j at error probability \varepsilon is at least \log(1/\varepsilon)/I_{ij}. Summing over hypotheses and using the threshold accept rule gives the bound. The human-delay overhead is additive because human actions execute after FIFO delay. \square

Remark 11.1. This gives "faster at equal safety" a theoretical footing: HARC reduces \mathbb{E}[T_{\text{resolution}}] by selecting tests that maximize Chernoff information, subject to the safety constraint.

Remark 11.2. The prior-art gap: active sequential hypothesis testing has not been combined with a trajectory-level chance constraint and a human-in-the-loop model in this exact form.

