Chapter 12: Cheap Validation via Monte Carlo

12.1 Synthetic Human

Definition 12.1 (Synthetic human). A synthetic human is a stochastic policy \pi_H^{\text{syn}} with known categorical parameters \boldsymbol{\mu}^* and known delay d^*.

12.2 Monte Carlo Check of Theorem 4.1

Procedure 12.1.

1. Sample K = 10^4 episodes with synthetic human.
2. At each step, sample failure F_t \sim \text{Bernoulli}(P(F \mid b_t, a_t)).
3. Record whether any failure occurs.
4. Estimate \hat{P}(\bigcup F_t) and compare to \varepsilon.

Proposition 12.1 (Concentration). By Hoeffding's inequality,

P\left( \left| \hat{P}\left(\bigcup F_t\right) - P\left(\bigcup F_t\right) \right| > \delta \right) \le 2 \exp(-2K\delta^2)

Remark 12.1. With K = 10^4 and \delta = 0.01, the bound is 2 \exp(-2) \approx 0.27, so the estimate is within 0.01 with high probability.

12.3 Monte Carlo Check of Robust Bound

Procedure 12.2.

1. Corrupt the model by \eta.
2. Run the Monte Carlo check under the corrupted model.
3. Estimate \hat{P}_{\mathcal{M}^*}(\bigcup F_t) and compare to \varepsilon + T \eta.

Remark 12.2. This validates Corollary 4.1 without physical tests or code training.

