Appendix C: Human Model — Full Derivation

C.1 Categorical Policy

Definition C.1 (Human policy). The human policy is

\pi_H(a^H \mid z, a^R) = \text{Cat}(a^H; \boldsymbol{\mu}(z, a^R))

where \boldsymbol{\mu}(z, a^R) \in \Delta^{K-1}.

Definition C.2 (Action space). A^H = \{\text{accept}, \text{reject}, \text{propose-alt}, \text{request-info}, \text{defer}, \text{no-op}\}.

Remark C.1. The no-op action handles non-response. It is distinct from defer (explicit postponement).

C.2 Dirichlet Conjugacy

Proposition C.1. The Dirichlet prior is conjugate to the Categorical likelihood. After observing n_k actions of type k in context (z, a^R),

P(\boldsymbol{\mu} \mid z, a^R, \{n_k\}) = \text{Dirichlet}(\boldsymbol{\alpha}(z, a^R) + \mathbf{n})

Proof. The likelihood is \prod_k \mu_k^{n_k}. The prior is \propto \prod_k \mu_k^{\alpha_k - 1}. Multiplying,

P(\boldsymbol{\mu} \mid \cdot) \propto \prod_k \mu_k^{\alpha_k + n_k - 1}

which is \text{Dirichlet}(\boldsymbol{\alpha} + \mathbf{n}). Normalization follows from the Dirichlet integral. \square

C.3 Posterior Predictive

Proposition C.2 (Posterior predictive).

P(a^H = k \mid z, a^R, \boldsymbol{\alpha}) = \frac{\alpha_k(z, a^R)}{\sum_j \alpha_j(z, a^R)}

Proof. \mathbb{E}[\mu_k \mid \boldsymbol{\alpha}] = \alpha_k / \sum_j \alpha_j by the Dirichlet mean formula. \square

C.4 Context Selection

Remark C.2. The Dirichlet is per observable context (z, a^R), not per hidden (s, z). The AI never observes s, so it cannot accumulate counts per s. The concentration parameters are sufficient statistics for the human policy in the observable context.

Proposition C.3 (Context sufficiency). The posterior predictive P(a^H = k \mid z, a^R, \boldsymbol{\alpha}) is a sufficient statistic for the human action distribution in context (z, a^R).

Proof. By conjugacy, the posterior \boldsymbol{\alpha}(z, a^R) is a sufficient statistic for the parameter \boldsymbol{\mu}(z, a^R). The posterior predictive depends on the data only through \boldsymbol{\alpha}. \square

