Appendix D: Queue Dynamics — Full Proof

D.1 Queue Definition

Definition D.1 (Queue). q_t = (a^H_{t-d+1}, \ldots, a^H_{t-1}) \in (A^H)^{d-1}.

Definition D.2 (Shift). \text{shift}(q_t, a^H_t) appends a^H_t and pops the oldest element if |q| \ge d-1.

Definition D.3 (Executed action). a^{\text{exec}}_t = \text{pop}(q_t) where \text{pop} returns the oldest element.

D.2 FIFO Correctness

Proposition D.1. Under Definitions D.1–D.3, the executed action at step t is the human action issued at step t - d.

Proof. By induction on t. At t=0, the queue is empty (no actions issued yet). At t=d, the action issued at step 0 is at the front of the queue and is popped. The inductive step: each shift appends the newly issued action and pops the oldest, maintaining FIFO ordering. \square

D.3 Queue-Augmented Transition

Definition D.4 (Joint transition).

T(s', z', q' \mid s, z, q, a^R) = \sum_{a^H} p(s' \mid s, a^R, a^{\text{exec}}) \, p(z' \mid z, a^R, a^{\text{exec}}) \, \pi_H(a^H \mid z, a^R) \, \mathbf{1}\{q' = \text{shift}(q, a^H)\}

Remark D.1. The transition feeds the executed action into p(s' \mid \cdot), not the newly issued action.

Proposition D.2 (Queue observability). The queue q_t is fully observable to the AI, since it contains human actions that the AI has already observed.

Proof. Human actions are part of the observation space O. The queue is a deterministic function of past observations and the AI's own actions. \square

