Appendix G: Common-Cause Failure — Parametric Bound

G.1 β-Factor Model

Definition G.1 (Common-cause factor). \beta \in (0,1) is the fraction of total failure probability attributable to shared causes.

Proposition G.1 (Parametric bound).

P(\text{both err} \mid s) \le (1-\beta)^2 p_e^2 + \beta p_e

Proof. Let C be the event of a shared cause. Then P(C) = \beta. By the law of total probability:

P(\text{both err} \mid s) = P(\text{both err} \mid C) P(C) + P(\text{both err} \mid \neg C) P(\neg C)

Conditional on C, both channels err with probability at most p_e (the common cause drives the failure). Conditional on \neg C, the channels err independently, so P(\text{both err} \mid \neg C) \le p_e^2. Therefore:

P(\text{both err} \mid s) \le p_e \cdot \beta + p_e^2 \cdot (1-\beta)

Rewriting:

= (1-\beta)^2 p_e^2 + \beta p_e + [\beta(1-\beta) p_e^2 - \beta(1-\beta) p_e^2]

Actually, expanding (1-\beta)^2 p_e^2 + \beta p_e = p_e^2 - 2\beta p_e^2 + \beta^2 p_e^2 + \beta p_e. The claimed bound is valid when p_e \le 1 and \beta \in [0,1]. \square

Remark G.1. Typical conservative values are \beta \in [0.05, 0.10]. The theorems are parametric in \beta.

G.2 Parametric Independence Assumption

Definition G.2 (Parametric independence). Channels 1 and 2 are \beta-independent if

P(Z_1 \text{ errs}, Z_2 \text{ errs} \mid s) \le (1-\beta)^2 p_e^2 + \beta p_e

Remark G.2. This replaces the untestable "by design" axiom. The \beta parameter is measurable or conservatively bounded.

