Appendix H: M0 Gate — Full Analysis

H.1 Discrepancy Statistic

Definition H.1 (KS discrepancy). T(s) = \sup_x |F_{\text{pred}}(x) - F_{\text{obs}}(x)|.

Remark H.1. The KS statistic is a standard choice for residual distribution comparison. Other choices (e.g., Anderson–Darling) may be more sensitive to tails.

H.2 Posterior Predictive p-Value

Definition H.2. p_{\text{PPP}} = P(T(s^{\text{rep}}) \ge T(s^{\text{obs}}) \mid \mathcal{M}, b).

Proposition H.1 (Concentration under composite null). Under a composite null with estimated parameters, p_{\text{PPP}} concentrates around 1/2, with less mass in both tails than a uniform distribution.

Proof. Robins, van der Vaart, and Ventura (2000) establish this for the posterior predictive p-value under a composite null. The asymptotic distribution is stochastically concentrated around 1/2, not uniform. \square

Corollary H.1. The false-positive rate of the M0 gate is lower than nominal (the test is conservative), but the power to detect misspecification is reduced.

H.3 M0 as Heuristic

Definition H.3 (M0 gate).

\text{M0}(b) = \begin{cases} \text{inadequate} & \text{if } p_{\text{PPP}} < \gamma_{\text{low}} \text{ or } p_{\text{PPP}} > \gamma_{\text{high}} \\ \text{adequate} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

Remark H.2. M0 is a heuristic, not a formal hypothesis test with guaranteed size. The thresholds are design parameters, calibrated empirically.

Remark H.3 (M0 role in robust soundness). M0 estimates \eta. If M0 triggers, the system outputs undemonstrated. The robust bound of Corollary B.1 states that guarantees are model-relative: P(\bigcup F_t) \le \varepsilon + T\eta.

