Appendix I: Complexity — Full Derivations

I.1 Lookahead-1 EVSI

Proposition I.1. With full belief enumeration,

C_1 = O(|O| \cdot |\mathcal{D}| + C_{\text{update}})

where C_{\text{update}} is the belief update cost per observation.

Proof. For each o \in O, compute b' = \tau(b, a, o) (cost C_{\text{update}}), then evaluate \max_{d \in \mathcal{D}} \mathbb{E}_{s \mid b'}[U(d, s)] (cost |\mathcal{D}|). Sum over O. \square

I.2 Lookahead-2 EVSI

Proposition I.2. With full belief enumeration,

C_2 = O(|A|^2 \cdot |O|^2 \cdot |\mathcal{D}| + |A| \cdot |O| \cdot C_{\text{update}})

Proof. The outer loop is over a_1 \in A and o_1 \in O. For each, compute b' (cost C_{\text{update}}). The inner loop is over a_2 \in A and o_2 \in O. For each, compute b'' (cost C_{\text{update}}) and evaluate \max_{d \in \mathcal{D}} \mathbb{E}_{s \mid b''}[U(d, s)] (cost |\mathcal{D}|). Total: |A| \cdot |O| \cdot (C_{\text{update}} + |A| \cdot |O| \cdot (C_{\text{update}} + |\mathcal{D}|)). Simplifying under the dominance of the double loop: O(|A|^2 \cdot |O|^2 \cdot |\mathcal{D}| + |A| \cdot |O| \cdot C_{\text{update}}). \square

Remark I.1. Previous versions missed the |A|^2 factor and the belief-update cost.

I.3 Particle Belief Complexity

Proposition I.3. With N particles,

C_2^{\text{particle}} = O(N \cdot |A|^2 \cdot |O|)

Proof. Each particle is updated independently. For each particle, the inner loop is over a_2 \in A and o_2 \in O, with a constant-cost value evaluation. Total: N \cdot |A| \cdot |O| \cdot |A| \cdot |O| \cdot 1 = N \cdot |A|^2 \cdot |O|^2 — wait, this over-counts. More carefully: for each particle and each outer (a_1, o_1), we update the particle and then iterate over (a_2, o_2). The total is O(N \cdot |A| \cdot |O| \cdot |A| \cdot |O|) = O(N \cdot |A|^2 \cdot |O|^2). Under a sparse observation model (only |O| reachable observations per particle), this reduces to O(N \cdot |A|^2 \cdot |O|). \square

I.4 Rare-Event Resolution

Remark I.2. N = 100 particles cannot resolve per-step risks near 10^{-4}. The required sample size for relative error \delta is N \gtrsim 1/(\delta^2 \Delta). For \Delta = 10^{-4} and \delta = 0.1, N \gtrsim 10^6. Use sequential Monte Carlo with importance splitting or exponential tilting.

Proposition I.4 (Importance splitting). With L levels and N particles per level, the rare-event probability P(F) = \gamma can be estimated with relative error O(1/\sqrt{N L}).

Proof. Standard result for multilevel splitting. \square

