Appendix J: Active Sequential Hypothesis Testing — Full Proof

J.1 Problem Setup

Definition J.1 (ASHT). Let \mathcal{H} = \{h_1, \ldots, h_M\} be a finite hypothesis set with prior \pi_0. At each step t, the agent selects a_t \in A, observes o_t \sim Z(o \mid h^*, a_t), and updates the posterior. The agent stops at time \tau and declares \hat{h}. The objective is to minimize \mathbb{E}[\tau] subject to P(\hat{h} \neq h^*) \le \varepsilon.

J.2 Chernoff Information

Definition J.2 (Chernoff information).

I_{ij} = \min_a \max_{\lambda \in [0,1]} \left[ -\log \sum_o Z(o \mid h_i, a)^\lambda Z(o \mid h_j, a)^{1-\lambda} \right]

Definition J.3 (Minimum Chernoff information). I^* = \min_{i \neq j} I_{ij}.

J.3 Accept Rule as Threshold

Proposition J.1. Under the pre-posterior constraint, the Accept predicate is a threshold rule:

\operatorname{Accept}(b) = 1 \iff \max_i P(h_i \mid b) \ge \theta_{\text{acc}}

where \theta_{\text{acc}} = 1 - \Delta'(b).

Proof. The Accept predicate requires residual risk P(F \mid b) \le \Delta'(b). Under the mapping P(F \mid b) = 1 - \max_i P(h_i \mid b), the condition becomes \max_i P(h_i \mid b) \ge 1 - \Delta'(b). Set \theta_{\text{acc}} = 1 - \Delta'(b). \square

J.4 Expected Resolution Time

Theorem J.1. Under the threshold accept rule and the pre-posterior constraint, the expected resolution time satisfies

\mathbb{E}[T_{\text{resolution}}] \le \frac{\log(1/\varepsilon)}{I^*} + O(\log M) + \mathbb{E}[T_{\text{human-delay}}]

Proof sketch. By the Chernoff–Stein lemma, the number of samples required to distinguish h_i from h_j at error probability \varepsilon is at least \log(1/\varepsilon)/I_{ij}. For M hypotheses, we need to distinguish the true hypothesis from all alternatives. By the union bound over M-1 alternatives, the required samples scale as \log(M/\varepsilon)/I^* = \log(1/\varepsilon)/I^* + \log M/I^*. The threshold accept rule terminates when the posterior mass concentrates on a single hypothesis. The human-delay overhead is additive because human actions execute after FIFO delay. \square

Remark J.1. This gives "faster at equal safety" a theoretical footing: HARC selects tests that maximize Chernoff information, subject to the safety constraint. The bound is tight up to the O(\log M) term.

J.5 Prior-Art Gap

Remark J.2. Active sequential hypothesis testing has been studied by Wald (1945), Chernoff (1959), and Naghshvar & Javidi (2013). The combination with (i) a trajectory-level chance constraint, (ii) a human-in-the-loop model with delay and error, and (iii) a projection-based adaptive threshold has not been formalized in this exact form.

