Appendix M: The HARC Optimization Problem — Formal Statement

M.1 Decision-Theoretic Formulation

Definition M.1 (HARC optimization).

\min_{\pi} \; \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{T} \gamma^t R(s_t, a_t) \right]

subject to:

\begin{aligned}
& P_\pi\left(\bigcup_{t=0}^{T} F_t \mid b_0\right) \le \varepsilon, \\
& a_t \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}(b_t) \; \forall t, \\
& \text{M0}(b_t) = \text{adequate} \; \forall t, \\
& \operatorname{Accept}(b_T, \mathcal{E}_T) = 1.
\end{aligned}

M.2 Relationship between R, C, U

Definition M.2 (Reward). R(s, a) = -C(s, a) - \lambda \cdot \mathbf{1}\{F_t \text{ imminent}\} for a Lagrange multiplier \lambda.

Definition M.3 (Utility). U(d, s) = \mathbb{E}_{s' \sim T(\cdot \mid s, d)}[R(s', d)].

Proposition M.1. The objective \min_\pi \mathbb{E}[\sum \gamma^t R] is equivalent to \max_\pi \mathbb{E}[\sum \gamma^t U] under the mapping of Definition M.3.

Proof. The reward at step t depends on the current state and action. The utility U(d, s) is the expected reward of taking action d in state s. Maximizing the expected cumulative reward is equivalent to maximizing the expected cumulative utility under the same discount. \square

Remark M.1. In previous versions, R, C, U were unrelated. This formulation relates them explicitly.

M.3 Lagrangian Relaxation

Definition M.4 (Lagrangian).

\mathcal{L}(\pi, \lambda) = \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[ \sum \gamma^t R \right] + \lambda \left( P_\pi\left(\bigcup F_t\right) - \varepsilon \right)

Remark M.2. The Lagrangian relaxation is standard for constrained MDPs. The dual problem \max_{\lambda \ge 0} \min_\pi \mathcal{L}(\pi, \lambda) gives a lower bound on the constrained optimum.

